dialogue_history.text:
{
  "response": "Yes—if the biopsy supports AITL, two classic histopathological findings you’d look for are a proliferation of arborizing high endothelial venules and an expansion of follicular dendritic cell meshworks, characteristically demonstrated by CD21 or CD23 immunostaining. If instead the infiltrate is dominated by large, atypical B cells that are EBV-encoded RNA (EBER) positive (as in EBV+ DLBCL) or shows an angiocentric/angiodestructive B-cell infiltrate with EBV positivity (as in lymphomatoid granulomatosis), that should prompt you to reconsider a B-cell lymphoma. Awaiting the histology is indeed key—once the diagnosis is established, main management considerations for AITL include multiagent chemotherapy (often CHOP-like regimens), possible consolidation with autologous stem cell transplant in eligible patients, and recognition that EBV-positive B-cell proliferations can emerge secondarily and rarely progress to an overt B-cell lymphoma.",
  "belief": "Cutaneous angioimmunoblastic T-cell lymphoma (AITL) is the most likely diagnosis, but EBV-driven B-cell lymphoproliferative disorders remain in the differential

